# Agent and reviewer scratch.
#
# These are per-machine working artefacts, not repository content, and leaving
# them untracked is not the same as leaving them alone: several agents work in
# this checkout, so anything unignored here reads as "the workspace has
# uncommitted changes" forever, and the next person to reach for `git add -A`
# sweeps somebody else's debris into a commit.
.impeccable/

# One-off analysis scripts. Sessions here routinely write a small script to
# compute a contrast ratio, sweep an inventory or probe an upstream, and those
# are working notes rather than repository content. This rule exists because
# they were not covered: commit e95c839 swept a reviewer's critique file into
# master, and by the time #77 added ignore rules the paths were already tracked,
# so the rules did nothing until they were untracked by hand. Cheaper to name
# the convention up front than to clean it up twice.
scratch-*
*.scratch.*
# `.tmp.` as well as `.scratch.`, because the convention above is only
# discoverable by reading this file. A session auditing mobile layout wrote
# `mobile-audit.tmp.mjs` and `shots.tmp.mjs` — a reasonable name that matched
# neither pattern, so two harnesses sat untracked and committable in a
# checkout several agents share. Naming a convention in .gitignore does not
# tell anyone it exists; covering both spellings does.
*.tmp.*

# Ad-hoc local preview config. The committed vite.config.ts is the real one;
# this variant proxies /api and /articles at the live site so a built frontend
# can be eyeballed against production data, which is a desk convenience rather
# than a repository artefact.
vite.preview-check.ts

